add('ma-pk-rhyme-circle','Rhyme Circle: Listen, Match, Invent','Massachusetts','Pre-K','Phonological awareness','Pre-K RF 2(a)','With adult support, recognize and generate spoken rhymes.',MA,'Small-group oral practice',
'A rhyme has the same ending sound, even when its spelling differs. Say each pair naturally, then stretch the ending. Children may answer by speaking, pointing to a yes/no card, or giving a thumbs signal. Adults read every word; printed-word reading is not expected. Accept a playful invented word if its ending genuinely rhymes, then model a real word. Use four prompts per sitting.',
[('Do moon and spoon rhyme?','Yes. Both end with the spoken /oon/ sound. The first sounds differ.'),('Give a rhyme for cake.','Lake, make, or bake works. Repeat the pair slowly so the ending can be heard.')],
[('Adult routine','Say: Listen first. Tell me if the endings sound the same. Then you will choose or invent a rhyme. Demonstrate YES and NOT YET response signals. Do not require spelling. For a three-word choice, repeat all three without emphasizing the answer.')],
[('Do boat and coat rhyme?','Yes; their ending sounds match.'),('Do sun and sock rhyme?','No; matching first sounds do not make a rhyme.'),('Which rhymes with fish: dish or fan?','Dish; fish/dish have matching endings.'),('Which rhymes with tree: bee or truck?','Bee; tree/bee end alike.'),('Do light and kite rhyme?','Yes; sound, not matching spelling, decides.'),('Do cup and cap rhyme?','No; their vowel sounds differ.'),('Which rhymes with snail: trail or snow?','Trail.'),('Which rhymes with chair: hair or shoe?','Hair.'),('Say a rhyme for day.','Examples: play, stay, hay. Accept any matching ending.'),('Say a rhyme for ring.','Examples: sing, king, wing.'),('Say a rhyme for red.','Examples: bed, head, said; spelling is irrelevant.'),('Say a rhyme for log.','Examples: dog or fog; accept the child’s consistent dialect.'),('Find the odd ending: bear, pear, boat.','Boat. Bear and pear rhyme.'),('Find the odd ending: nest, best, nose.','Nose. Nest and best rhyme.'),('Finish a silly line with a rhyme: A mouse sat by a ___.','House is one answer. Accept another genuine rhyme; meaning may be silly.'),('Make your own two-word rhyme pair.','Accept two distinct words with the same ending sound, such as blue/shoe.') ,('Do rain and train rhyme?','Yes.'),('Which rhymes with bug: rug or bag?','Rug.'),('Say a rhyme for hop.','Examples: top, mop, stop.'),('Which does not rhyme: seed, need, sand?','Sand.')],early=True,price=400)

add('ma-k-story-path','Story Path: Retell Two Tiny Adventures','Massachusetts','Kindergarten','Reading / oral language','RL.K.2','Retell key events from a familiar read-aloud with prompting and support.',MA,'Read-aloud classwork',
'Read one story aloud twice before questioning. A retell says what happened, in order, and includes the important problem and ending. Model first, next, and finally. Children may speak, draw, or act. Adults record dictated words; do not score handwriting or decoding.',
[('Retell: Jo lost a glove. Jo looked under a chair. The glove was there.','First Jo lost a glove. Next Jo looked under a chair. Finally Jo found it.'),('Is “Jo had a glove” a complete retell?','No. Add the missing problem, search, and discovery.')],
[('A. The Paper Boat','Mina folded a paper boat. She set it in a shallow tray of water. The boat tipped, and Mina sighed. She dried it and made a wider bottom. This time the boat stayed up. Mina called her brother to see it float.'),('B. A Place for the Seed','Omar found a bean seed in the class seed jar. He put it on a dry plate. “It needs a place to grow,” said his teacher. Omar filled a cup with soil. He planted the seed and watered it. He placed the cup by the window and made a name label.')],
[('A: Who made the boat?','Mina.'),('A: What did Mina do first?','Folded a paper boat.'),('A: What happened in the water?','The boat tipped.'),('A: How did Mina change the boat?','She dried it and made its bottom wider.'),('A: What happened after the change?','It stayed up and floated.'),('A: Whom did Mina call?','Her brother.'),('A: Put these in order: wider bottom; fold boat; boat tips.','Fold boat, boat tips, wider bottom.'),('A: Retell with first, next, and finally.','Include folding, tipping, widening, and floating. Accept speech or ordered drawings.'),('B: Who found the seed?','Omar.'),('B: Where was the seed first?','In the class seed jar.'),('B: Where did Omar put it before planting?','On a dry plate.'),('B: What did the teacher say it needed?','A place to grow.'),('B: Name two things Omar did with the cup.','Filled it with soil; planted and watered the seed. Any two.'),('B: Where did he put the planted cup?','By the window.'),('B: What did he make at the end?','A name label.'),('B: Retell the seed’s journey.','Jar, dry plate, soil-filled cup, water, window/label; prioritize the main sequence.'),('Choose A or B. Tell the beginning.','A: Mina folds a boat. B: Omar finds a seed in the jar.'),('Tell the main problem or need.','A: The boat tips. B: The seed needs a place to grow.'),('Tell what the character did about it.','A: Makes a wider bottom. B: Plants and waters the seed in soil.'),('Tell the ending without adding a new event.','A: Boat floats and Mina calls her brother. B: Cup goes by the window with a label; the story does not say it sprouts.')],early=True,price=400)

add('il-g1-digraph-workshop','Two Letters, One Sound: SH and CH Workshop','Illinois','1','Phonics','CC.1.R.F.3.a','Read common sh and ch consonant digraphs in words and short sentences.',IL,'Phonics independent practice',
'In these words, sh works together for one sound, as in shop. Ch works together for one sound, as in chin. Point under the two letters together; blend the remaining sounds. Do not add an extra uh to consonants. This packet covers sh/ch, not every digraph. An adult reads directions, not the target word during the initial attempt.',
[('Read shell. Point to the digraph.','sh is the digraph. Blend /sh/ /e/ /l/; ll represents the final /l/.'),('Complete __op for a place to buy things.','shop: use sh. Chop is a different word and does not match this clue.')],
[('Read-and-check bank','sh: shop, ship, shed, shut, wish, fish, dish, rush. ch: chin, chip, chop, chat, rich, much. Say the word, touch the digraph, then use the word in a short spoken sentence. Adult: explain unfamiliar meanings after the decoding attempt.')],
[('Read ship. Name its first two letters and their sound.','sh; one /sh/ sound. Then blend ship.'),('Read chin. Name its first two letters and their sound.','ch; one /ch/ sound. Then blend chin.'),('Read fish. Is its digraph first or last?','sh is last.'),('Read rich. Is its digraph first or last?','ch is last.'),('Sort shop, chat, dish, chip by sh or ch.','sh: shop, dish. ch: chat, chip.'),('Sort shut, much, wish, chop by sh or ch.','sh: shut, wish. ch: much, chop.'),('Complete __ip for a boat: sh or ch? Then read it.','ship, sh.'),('Complete __in for the part below your mouth.','chin, ch.'),('Complete fi__ for an animal in water.','fish, sh.'),('Complete ri__ for having a lot of money.','rich, ch.'),('Read: A fish is in a dish. Name both sh words.','fish and dish; read the sentence accurately.'),('Read: I can chat in the shop. Name one ch word and one sh word.','chat and shop.'),('Change sh in ship to ch. Read both words.','ship becomes chip; the vowel and final consonant stay the same.'),('Change ch in chop to sh. Read both words.','chop becomes shop.'),('Write one sh word from the bank and read it.','Any listed sh word, accurately written and read.'),('Write a short sentence using chip. Read it.','Example: I got a chip. Accept a meaningful sentence with chip decoded accurately.'),('Read shed and point to the digraph.','sh at the beginning.'),('Read much and point to the digraph.','ch at the end.'),('Complete wi__ for something you hope for.','wish, sh.'),('Read: The rich cat can shop. Find the ch and sh words.','rich has ch; shop has sh. Read the full sentence.')],price=450)

add('il-g2-plural-detectives','Plural Detectives: More Than One','Illinois','2','Grammar / spelling','CC.2.L.1.b','Form and use frequent irregular plural nouns in sentences.',IL,'Grammar warm-ups',
'Many plural nouns add s, but irregular plurals change differently or stay the same. Use the bank to learn the forms, then cover it for the exit check. A plural tells how many; it does not show ownership. Use these tasks in four short practice sessions.',
[('One tooth; three ___.','teeth. Tooth changes internally; tooths is not the standard plural.'),('One deer; five ___.','deer. The singular and plural forms are the same.')],
[('Reference bank','child → children; foot → feet; tooth → teeth; mouse → mice; person → people; goose → geese; man → men; woman → women; deer → deer; sheep → sheep. These are common classroom forms; people is the ordinary plural of person here.')],
[('One child; four ___.','children.'),('One foot; two ___.','feet.'),('One mouse; three ___.','mice.'),('One goose; six ___.','geese.'),('One woman; several ___.','women.'),('One man; two ___.','men.'),('One person; many ___.','people.'),('One sheep; seven ___.','sheep.'),('Fix: The childs brought books.','The children brought books.'),('Fix: Two gooses crossed the path.','Two geese crossed the path.'),('Fix: My foots fit these shoes.','My feet fit these shoes.'),('Fix: The mouses hid behind a box.','The mice hid behind a box.'),('Choose: The two (deer/deers) stood still.','deer.'),('Choose: The (womans/women) opened the gate.','women.'),('Write a sentence about more than one tooth.','Example: My teeth are clean. Require teeth used as a plural noun.'),('Write a sentence with people.','Example: Three people waited. Require plural meaning and a complete sentence.'),('One goose; four ___.','geese.'),('Fix: Two sheeps rest here.','Two sheep rest here.'),('Choose: Both (child/children) waved.','children.'),('Write one sentence using mice correctly.','Example: The mice found crumbs. Require more than one mouse and a complete thought.')],price=450)

add('mi-g2-lantern-lesson','The Lantern Helpers: Recount and Find a Lesson','Michigan','2','Reading','RL.2.2','Recount an original story and connect its central lesson to key events.',MI,'Reading classwork',
'A topic is a short label, such as helping. A lesson is an idea a reader can use, such as asking for help can solve a problem. Retell the main events before naming the lesson. This original realistic story gives focused practice; it is not a folktale or a complete study of the standard’s range of cultures and genres.',
[('A character shares a pencil, and both children finish. What lesson fits?','Sharing can help people succeed. The successful result supports that lesson.'),('Is “a pencil” a lesson?','No. It names an object, not a useful idea learned from events.')],
[('The Lantern Helpers','Pia wanted to make a paper lantern for the class display. She cut a long strip, but it curled into a tight tube. She pulled it flat. It curled again.\n“I can hold one end,” said Ren.\n“I want to do it myself,” Pia replied. She tried to tape both ends with one hand. The tape stuck to her sleeve.\nRen waited beside her. Pia looked at the sticky sleeve and began to laugh. “Please hold this side.” Ren held the paper while Pia placed the tape. The lantern stayed round.\nRen picked up a second strip. “Now mine keeps curling.” Pia held it steady. Together they finished two lanterns. Pia wrote both names on the display card. Her lantern was still her idea, but the work no longer had to be hers alone.')],
[('What does Pia want to make?','A paper lantern for the class display.'),('What makes the task difficult?','The strip curls and she cannot easily hold both ends while taping.'),('What help does Ren first offer?','To hold one end.'),('Why does Pia first refuse?','She wants to do it herself.'),('What happens to the tape?','It sticks to her sleeve.'),('What does Pia do before asking for help?','Looks at her sleeve and laughs.'),('How does Ren help solve the problem?','Holds the paper while Pia tapes it.'),('How does Pia later help Ren?','Holds Ren’s curling strip steady.'),('Order: Pia helps Ren; tape sticks; Ren offers help.','Ren offers help; tape sticks; Pia helps Ren.'),('Retell the beginning in one sentence.','Pia tries to make a lantern but the paper curls.'),('Retell the middle in one sentence.','She first refuses help, then asks Ren to hold the paper.'),('Retell the ending in one sentence.','They help each other finish two lanterns and Pia writes both names.'),('Choose a lesson: Work must always be alone; Help can make a hard job easier.','Help can make a hard job easier; the lanterns are finished after cooperation.'),('Which event best supports that lesson?','Ren holds the paper so Pia can tape it; accept the matching later event with Pia helping.'),('Why does the card with both names matter?','Pia recognizes that both children helped, showing her change.'),('Explain how Pia changes.','From insisting on working alone to asking for and giving help, supported by these actions.'),('Name the main problem.','Curling paper makes the lantern hard to tape alone.'),('Name the action that solves it.','One child holds the paper while the other tapes.'),('State a lesson as a full sentence.','Accept a supported idea about cooperation or accepting help, not only the topic “lanterns.”'),('Retell three key events in order to support your lesson.','Pia struggles/refuses help; Ren holds paper after she asks; Pia helps Ren and credits both. Other accurate sequences accepted.')],price=500)

add('mi-g3-recess-letter','A Better Recess Corner: Opinion Letter Workshop','Michigan','3','Writing','W.3.1','Write an opinion with organized reasons, linking language, and a conclusion.',MI,'Writing workshop',
'An opinion states what you think should happen. Reasons explain why. Introduce the topic, state one clear opinion, group useful reasons, connect them with because/also/therefore, and end by returning to your opinion. Use the fictional planning notes without claiming they describe your own school.',
[('Improve “The corner.” into an opinion.','Our class should create a quiet reading corner for recess. It names the topic and a position.'),('Connect: It gives another activity. Some students prefer quiet play.','We should offer a reading corner because some students prefer quiet play. The reason supports the opinion.')],
[('Fictional planning notes','Pine Class may choose one indoor recess corner: a puzzle table or a drawing table. Both fit four children. The puzzle table has six reusable puzzles. Its pieces need counting after use. The drawing table has scrap paper and shared pencils. Children can display finished drawings. Both spaces need a cleanup routine. These are invented classroom facts for this writing exercise.')],
[('Name the decision Pine Class must make.','Choose a puzzle table or a drawing table.'),('Write your opinion in a complete sentence.','Either choice is acceptable if clearly stated.'),('Give one reason for your choice.','Example: Drawing lets children create pictures; or puzzles can be used repeatedly.'),('Give a second, different reason.','Example: Drawing displays let children share art; puzzles provide six choices. Must be distinct and supported.'),('Which is more useful: “I like it” or a detail about what children can do? Why?','A specific activity detail explains a benefit; “I like it” repeats preference without explaining.'),('Write an opening naming the topic and opinion.','Example: For indoor recess, Pine Class should add a drawing table.'),('Use because to connect one reason to your opinion.','Complete sentence with a logically relevant reason.'),('Use also to add the second reason.','Sentence adds a genuinely different benefit.'),('Identify one cleanup need for your choice.','Puzzle pieces need counting; shared drawing supplies need gathering. Accept reasonable inference labeled as a plan.'),('Explain how a cleanup routine could address that need.','Example: Assign a piece checker or a pencil collector; explain the connection.'),('Write a conclusion returning to your opinion.','Example: For these reasons, a puzzle table would be a useful recess choice.'),('Should your conclusion suddenly ask for a pet? Explain.','No; that is a new topic not supported by the letter’s reasons.'),('Plan the order: reasons, conclusion, opening.','Opening, reasons, conclusion.'),('Draft a 5–7-sentence letter using your work.','Require topic/opinion, two relevant reasons, linking language, and conclusion. Either choice may earn full credit.'),('Underline a linking word in your letter and explain its job.','Because connects a reason; also adds support; therefore signals a conclusion.'),('Revise one vague sentence to be more specific.','Example: “It is fun” → “Six puzzles give the group several choices.” Require an observable improvement.'),('New choice: a class read-aloud or a class drawing time. State an opinion.','Either clear preference with the topic stated.'),('Give two reasons that support it.','Require two relevant, different reasons; personal experience may be identified as personal.'),('Connect your reasons using a linking word or phrase.','Meaningful because, also, since, or for example; sentence remains coherent.'),('Write a short concluding sentence.','Returns to the stated choice without introducing an unrelated demand.')],price=550)

add('md-g3-word-jobs','Word Jobs: Nouns, Verbs, Adjectives, and Adverbs','Maryland','3','Grammar','L1.a (Grade 3)','Explain how nouns, pronouns, verbs, adjectives, and adverbs function in sentences.',MD+'Grade-3-Language-A.pdf','Grammar classwork',
'A word’s job depends on its sentence. A noun names; a pronoun can replace a noun; a verb expresses an action or state; an adjective describes a noun; an adverb can describe how an action happens. Ask what the word does here, not just what ending it has. This packet uses clear examples, not all possible word jobs.',
[('In “The small bird sings softly,” explain small and softly.','Small describes bird, so it is an adjective. Softly describes how the bird sings, so it is an adverb.'),('In “Lena smiles. She waves,” what does she do?','She is a pronoun standing for Lena, avoiding repetition of the name.')],
[('Word-job reference','Noun: garden, dancer, hope. Pronoun: he, she, it, they. Verb: hops, is, became. Adjective: narrow, bright, patient. Adverb: slowly, carefully, often. Not every adverb ends in ly, and not every ly word is an adverb. Use the sentence to explain the relationship.')],
[('In “The rabbit hops,” name the noun.','rabbit names the animal.'),('In “The rabbit hops,” name the verb.','hops tells the action.'),('What does blue do in “A blue kite rises”?','Describes kite; adjective.'),('What does gently do in “Rain falls gently”?','Describes how rain falls; adverb.'),('Replace Nora in the second sentence: Nora reads. Nora laughs.','Nora reads. She laughs. She replaces Nora.'),('Replace the boxes: The boxes are heavy. The boxes need a cart.','They need a cart. They refers to the boxes.'),('Explain quiet in “The quiet room has a rug.”','Adjective describing room.'),('Explain quietly in “We enter quietly.”','Adverb describing how we enter.'),('Add an adjective: The ___ path bends. Explain its job.','Example: narrow; describes path. Accept an appropriate adjective.'),('Add an adverb: The turtle moves ___. Explain its job.','Example: slowly; describes moves.'),('In “They build a tall tower,” identify they and tower.','They is a pronoun; tower is a noun naming a thing.'),('In “The sky is gray,” is is a verb even without movement?','Yes. Is expresses a state/link between sky and gray.'),('Explain light in “The light bag lifts easily.”','Adjective describing bag as not heavy.'),('Explain light in “The light shines.”','Noun naming a source of illumination; job differs from item 13.'),('Write a sentence with a noun and an action verb. Label each.','Example: Birds [noun] glide [verb]. Labels must match actual use.'),('Add an adjective and an adverb to that sentence.','Example: Small birds glide smoothly. Small describes birds; smoothly describes glide.'),('In “He carries a red box carefully,” name the pronoun and noun.','He is the pronoun; box is the noun.'),('Name the verb in that sentence.','carries.'),('Explain red and carefully in that sentence.','Red describes box, adjective; carefully describes carries, adverb.'),('Change carefully to quickly. What changes?','The manner of carrying changes from taking care to moving fast; the carrier and box remain the same.')],price=500)

add('md-g4-three-forms','One Missing Bell, Three Literary Forms','Maryland','4','Reading / drama / poetry','RL.4.5','Compare how prose, drama, and poetry use structural features to tell an event.',MD+'Grade-4-Reading-Literature-A.pdf','Small-group genre lab',
'Prose commonly uses sentences and paragraphs; drama gives actors dialogue and stage directions; poetry uses purposeful lines and may group them in stanzas. Not all poems rhyme. Compare the effect of each form, not just its appearance. Read all three original versions below.',
[('How can a script show hesitation without a narrator?','Use a stage direction such as [pauses at the door]. It guides an actor’s action.'),('Does a line break make a sentence ungrammatical?','No. A poem may spread one sentence across several lines to shape emphasis or pace.')],
[('A. Prose: The Bell','Ari reached for the bell that signaled cleanup, but the hook was empty. He searched under the art table. Beside a brush lay a scrap of silver ribbon. Ari followed another scrap to a cardboard castle. Inside it, Bea was fitting the bell into a pretend tower. “May the tower borrow it after cleanup?” she asked. Ari smiled and held out his hand.'),('B. Drama: The Bell','Cast: Ari, Bea. Setting: an art room.\nARI: [checks the empty hook] Where is our bell?\nBEA: [inside a cardboard castle] Listen to my tower!\nARI: We need it for cleanup.\nBEA: [offers the bell] May the tower borrow it afterward?\nARI: [smiles] Afterward.'),('C. Poem: The Bell','An empty hook,\na waiting hand—\na silver trail\nacross the land.\n\nA cardboard tower\nborrows a sound;\nwe ring for cleanup,\nthen pass it around.')],
[('Which version uses a cast list?','B, drama.'),('Which uses two stanzas?','C, poem.'),('Which tells most events through a narrator’s sentences?','A, prose.'),('Quote one stage direction from B and explain its purpose.','Example: “[checks the empty hook]” tells the actor an action that reveals the problem.'),('How many lines are in C’s first stanza?','Four.'),('Name a rhyming pair in C.','hand/land or sound/around.'),('What does the blank line in C separate?','Two stanzas; it groups the discovery and the resolution.'),('How does B show who is speaking?','Speaker names before each line of dialogue.'),('What detail about Ari’s search appears only in A?','He searches under the art table and follows ribbon scraps; accept a specific unique detail.'),('Which version explicitly ends with sharing the bell?','C: “then pass it around.” A and B imply future borrowing, but do not state passing it around.'),('What would actors do with “[offers the bell]”?','Bea would physically offer the bell to Ari.'),('How does “a waiting hand” differ from “Ari reached”?','Poem uses a compact image; prose names Ari and tells the action directly.'),('Describe one similarity in the events across all versions.','A bell is in a cardboard tower and needed for cleanup.'),('Explain one structural difference between A and B.','A uses narrated paragraphs; B uses named speakers and actor directions.'),('Turn one sentence from A into a script line and direction.','Example: ARI: [looks beneath table] I found a ribbon. Must separate speech/action coherently.'),('Write two poem lines about the bell; explain a line-break choice.','Accept original lines and a plausible explanation of emphasis/pause. Rhyming is optional.'),('Which form gives actors directions? Name two features.','Drama; cast/speaker labels and stage directions.'),('Which two features identify C as poetry?','Intentional line breaks and stanzas; rhyme also acceptable.'),('Does prose never contain dialogue? Explain using A.','No. A includes Bea’s spoken question inside narrated prose.'),('Explain how one form changes the reader’s experience of the event.','Example: B makes us imagine a performance, while A lets a narrator lead us through the search. Require a structural feature and its effect.')],price=550)

add('ct-g4-inference-notebook','The Unsent Note: An Inference Notebook','Connecticut','4','Reading','4.RL.1','Support explanations and inferences with details from an original story.',CT,'Independent reading practice',
'An inference joins a text clue to reasonable thinking. It is not a guess with no evidence. Separate what the story states from what you infer. Use a short quote or accurate paraphrase and explain why it supports your idea.',
[('A child places wet boots by a dripping coat. Infer the weather.','It likely rained; both wet boots and dripping coat support the inference. The exact weather is not stated.'),('Can you infer the child’s favorite color?','No. Nothing in those details supports a color preference.')],
[('The Unsent Note','[1] June folded a note three times and slid it beneath her notebook. Across the classroom, Milo arranged the bridge model they had built together. One side leaned toward the table.\n[2] “It was straight yesterday,” Milo said. June pressed her palms beneath her legs. At lunch she had moved the model to make space for her painting, and a sleeve had caught the support string. She had hoped the tilt was too small to notice.\n[3] Their teacher announced that the models would be tested after recess. June took out the note. It began, “I need to tell you something.” She looked at Milo, who was searching the floor for a missing support clip.\n[4] June stood. “The string caught on my sleeve when I moved it,” she said. “I should have told you.” Milo stopped searching. Then he set two clips beside the bridge. “Hold this side while I tighten it.”\n[5] After the test, June unfolded the note. On the back she wrote, “Next time, sooner.” She left it inside her notebook where she would see it in the morning.')],
[('What is explicitly wrong with the bridge in paragraph 1?','One side leans toward the table.'),('What caused the damage?','June’s sleeve caught the support string while she moved the model.'),('Infer why June hides the note at first. Cite a clue.','She is reluctant or anxious to admit fault; she hides it and hopes the tilt will not be noticed.'),('What does pressing her palms beneath her legs suggest?','Nervousness or discomfort; connect the gesture to her concealed accident, not certain knowledge of her feelings.'),('Why does the test announcement matter to June?','It creates urgency: the damaged bridge will soon be tested, so keeping silent may harm their work.'),('What might the note have been intended to do? Use evidence.','Tell Milo about the accident; its opening says she needs to tell him something.'),('Is Milo shown blaming June before she speaks? Explain.','No. He notices the tilt and searches for a clip; blame is not stated.'),('What does Milo’s response suggest about his immediate priority?','Repairing the bridge together; he asks June to hold it while he tightens it.'),('Choose the better inference: June dislikes painting; June regrets waiting. Give evidence.','June regrets waiting; “I should have told you” and “Next time, sooner” support it.'),('Is the bridge guaranteed to pass the test?','No. The outcome is not stated; “after the test” only tells time.'),('Give two details showing June takes responsibility.','She identifies her sleeve as the cause and says she should have told Milo.'),('Explain what “Next time, sooner” most likely means.','Admit an accident or problem earlier rather than hiding it.'),('Why keep the note where she will see it?','Likely as a reminder to speak honestly sooner; supported by placement for the next morning.'),('Distinguish one stated fact and one inference.','Fact: she writes on the back. Inference: the note is a reminder. Require clear distinction.'),('Write a two-sentence evidence-based explanation of June’s change.','From concealment to admitting responsibility; cite hiding/hope and confession/reminder.'),('Name one question the text leaves unanswered.','Examples: whether the bridge passed or exactly how Milo felt. Do not supply an invented answer.'),('Infer June’s feeling before confession and cite a detail.','Anxiety/regret with hidden note or hand gesture; other supported feelings accepted.'),('Infer Milo’s response to working with June after the confession.','He is willing to cooperate; asks her to hold the bridge.'),('Explain why the final note matters.','It turns an unused confession note into a personal reminder about honesty/timing.'),('Reject this claim with evidence: June never tells Milo what happened.','Paragraph 4 explicitly records her explanation about the sleeve and apology.')],price=550)

add('ct-g4-opinion-evidence','Choose the Class Exhibit: Opinion with Evidence','Connecticut','4','Writing','4.W.1','Organize an opinion and support reasons with provided facts and details.',CT,'Writing small-group workshop',
'Use an opinion, organized reasons, supporting details, linking phrases, and a conclusion. A reason tells why; a fact or detail makes that reason concrete. Both choices below can produce a strong response. Evaluate the writing’s support, not whether you agree with the choice.',
[('Reason: “A map exhibit is interactive.” Add support from invented facts: visitors can move labels.','A map exhibit is interactive because visitors can place the movable labels on the map.'),('Is “Everyone will love it” supported by that fact?','No. A feature does not prove every visitor’s reaction. Use a limited claim such as visitors can participate.')],
[('Fictional exhibit brief','Oak Class has one display table and a budget of $30. A Weather Journal exhibit costs $18: it uses ten student observation pages and three hand-drawn charts. Visitors can compare patterns. A Story Design exhibit costs $24: it uses six original story openings and reusable character cards. Visitors can combine cards to suggest new plots. Either exhibit fits the table. The class has two afternoons to prepare. These are invented planning details, not research findings.')],
[('State the choice and audience for an opinion letter.','Choose the class exhibit; audience could be teacher/class planning team.'),('Write a clear opinion.','Either exhibit, named in a complete position statement.'),('List two reasons for your choice.','Example Weather: lower cost, comparison activity. Story: creative participation, reuse of cards. Both must fit brief.'),('Match one fact to each reason.','Weather: $18/$30, ten pages/three charts; Story: plot-card interaction, reusable cards. Require actual support.'),('Which exhibit costs less, and by how much?','Weather Journal costs $6 less: 24−18=6.'),('Does lower cost alone prove better learning? Explain.','No. Cost supports affordability, not an automatic learning outcome.'),('Write a topic sentence for your first reason.','Example: The Weather Journal keeps spending low. Must connect to opinion.'),('Add a factual supporting sentence.','Example: It costs $18 of our $30 budget.'),('Write a second reason paragraph of two sentences.','Clear different reason plus a relevant detail from brief.'),('Connect your reasons with in addition or for instance.','In addition adds a reason; for instance introduces evidence. Require correct logical use.'),('Correct: “The Story Design costs $18.”','The Story Design costs $24.'),('Improve: “Every visitor will become a great author.”','Example: Visitors can practice suggesting plots with character cards. Avoid unsupported guarantee.'),('A classmate prefers the other choice. Name one real advantage it has.','Weather lower cost/charts; Story creative interactive/reusable cards. Fairly represent other option.'),('Write a conclusion related to your opinion.','Restates recommendation and strongest reason without new unsupported claims.'),('Draft a two-paragraph letter using at least three facts.','Require opinion, grouped reasons, ≥3 accurate brief details, linking language, and conclusion.'),('Audit your letter: mark each fact and revise an unsupported claim.','Accept correctly marked details and a revision limiting/removing an overclaim.') ,('New brief: a timeline display costs $10; an audio display costs $22. Budget is $20. Recommend one.','Timeline is affordable; audio exceeds budget unless changed. Other recommendation must acknowledge constraint.'),('Give one reason and its supporting fact.','Timeline fits budget because $10≤$20; or audio would need at least $2 cost reduction.'),('Write a sentence linking the reason to the opinion.','Coherent because/therefore/for instance link with accurate amounts.'),('Write a fitting conclusion.','Returns to recommendation; no invented promise about learning or popularity.')],price=550)

add('oh-g5-context-lab','Context Lab: Meanings Beyond One Sentence','Ohio','5','Vocabulary','L.5.4','Infer and verify a word meaning using surrounding context.',OH,'Vocabulary warm-ups',
'Read before and after the target word. Look for examples, contrasts, causes, and explanations. Substitute a possible meaning and reread. Context may narrow a meaning without proving it; use the supplied mini-glossary for final verification. Focus is the context strand of the revised 2026 standard, not every strategy in the full standard.',
[('The fragile tile broke when a spoon tapped it. What does fragile mean?','Easily broken. The light tap causing a break supports that meaning.'),('Is “colorful” a supported meaning for fragile?','No. The sentence describes breakability, not color.')],
[('Verification glossary','reluctant: unwilling or hesitant; ample: more than enough; scarce: in short supply; precise: exact; temporary: lasting a limited time; sturdy: strongly built; revise: change to improve; dormant: inactive for a time; abrupt: sudden; brisk: quick and energetic. Use the glossary after attempting a meaning from clues.')],
[('Nia was reluctant to step onto the stage. She stayed at the doorway until her partner offered to begin. Infer reluctant and cite a clue.','Hesitant/unwilling; staying at the doorway is a clue.'),('We packed ample paper. Even after everyone made two posters, half the stack remained. Infer ample.','More than enough; much remained after all used it.'),('Water was scarce at the camp. Each group received only a small container. Infer scarce.','In short supply; only a small amount per group.'),('The cut had to be precise. A crooked edge would stop the parts from fitting. Infer precise.','Exact; fit depends on the edge’s accuracy.'),('The temporary sign stayed up for one afternoon. A permanent metal one arrived next day. Infer temporary.','Lasting a limited time; contrast with permanent and one afternoon.'),('The sturdy shelf held every heavy box without bending. Infer sturdy.','Strongly built; supports heavy weight.'),('Jae will revise the instructions. Several readers missed the same step, so he plans clearer wording. Infer revise.','Change to improve; plans clearer wording after a problem.'),('The club remained dormant during the holiday. Meetings began again in autumn. Infer dormant here.','Inactive for a time; meetings paused and resumed.'),('The music ended abruptly. One moment it played; the next, silence. What does abruptly mean?','Suddenly; immediate shift. It is the adverb form of abrupt.'),('We took a brisk walk, moving quickly enough to arrive before the bell. Infer brisk.','Quick and energetic; moving quickly is explicit support.'),('In item 3, would abundant fit scarce? Explain.','No. Abundant means plentiful, opposite to the small supply described.'),('In item 4, could precise mean expensive? Explain.','No. Accuracy/fitting is supported; no price information appears.'),('Verify reluctant and temporary using the glossary; name each supporting clue.','Reluctant=hesitant (doorway); temporary=limited duration (one afternoon).'),('Write a two-sentence context for sturdy without using strong.','Example: The sturdy stool held the heavy pot. Its legs did not wobble. Require meaning-revealing clue.'),('Write a contrast context for scarce using plenty in the second sentence.','Example: Glue was scarce today. Yesterday we had plenty. Contrast must clarify shortage.'),('Explain why a guessed meaning should be reread in the sentence.','To check that it fits grammar, surrounding details, and overall meaning; guesses can be unsupported.'),('The route was temporary: workers reopened the usual road on Friday. Infer temporary and cite a clue.','Limited duration; usual route returns Friday.'),('Tess gave precise directions: turn left at the second gate, not the first. Explain precise.','Exact; specific gate and direction remove ambiguity.'),('The stack was ample; every team took ten sheets and many remained. Explain ample.','More than enough; all teams supplied and surplus remains.'),('Write an original context for revise and explain the clue you used.','Requires changing something to improve it, e.g., instructions corrected after feedback, plus explanation.')],price=500)

add('oh-g6-window-seat','The Window Seat: Narrator Perspective Lab','Ohio','6','Reading','RL.6.6','Explain how first-person narration develops a narrator’s perspective.',OH,'Reading homework / classwork',
'Point of view describes the narration; perspective describes the narrator’s outlook. A first-person narrator tells only what they observe, remember, or think, not everyone’s inner thoughts. Trace word choices and selected details. A narrator may revise an interpretation when new evidence appears.',
[('“I called the delay a disaster; my sister called it a break.” What can you infer?','The speaker views the delay negatively, while the sister’s description suggests a benefit. Their labels reveal perspectives.'),('Does first person make every interpretation true?','No. It gives access to the narrator’s interpretation, which can be incomplete or mistaken.')],
[('The Window Seat','[1] I claimed the window seat before our club’s bus trip. A perfect view, I thought, and no one talking over my sketches. When Lena sat beside me with a folded map, I pictured a long morning of interruptions.\n[2] At the first turn she lifted a finger toward the glass. “That old station—”\n“I’m drawing,” I said. She folded the map smaller. For the next ten minutes I filled my page with rooflines and ignored the quiet beside me.\n[3] The bus stopped at a blocked road. From my precious window I could see only a wall. Lena opened her map, then closed it when I glanced over. On its back was a careful drawing of the station, with three different roof shapes labeled.\n[4] “You sketch buildings?” I asked. She nodded. “My grandfather helped repair that roof. I wanted to show you the curved beams.”\n[5] I moved my notebook between us. The wall had become less interesting. When the bus finally turned, Lena pointed out a row of arches I would have missed. I still had the window seat, but now the view felt wider.')],
[('Identify the narrative point of view and a pronoun that proves it.','First person; I/my/us as narrator participates.'),('What does the narrator initially want from the seat?','An uninterrupted view for sketching.'),('How does “interruptions” frame Lena before she speaks much?','As an obstacle/nuisance, revealing the narrator’s assumption.'),('What can we directly know about Lena’s thoughts?','Only what she says or displays; narrator does not directly access her thoughts.'),('Why does the narrator mention ignoring the quiet?','Shows deliberate withdrawal and focus on own activity, making later change visible.'),('How is “precious window” ironic when the bus stops?','The highly valued view now shows only a wall; the supposedly useful seat offers little.'),('Which detail first challenges the narrator’s assumption?','Lena’s careful labeled station drawing reveals a shared interest.'),('What does Lena’s family connection add?','She has specific knowledge of the roof’s repair, not merely an urge to interrupt.'),('Why does moving the notebook between them matter?','An action showing willingness to share and collaborate.'),('Explain “the view felt wider.”','Metaphorically, shared knowledge broadens what the narrator notices; window itself has not grown.'),('Track one word/detail from before and after the change.','Before: interruptions/ignored quiet. After: notebook between us/arches/wider. Explain changing openness.'),('Can we conclude Lena intended to annoy the narrator?','No; the map/drawing and explanation support an interest in sharing architecture.'),('How might Lena narrate paragraph 2 differently?','Might describe disappointment or uncertainty after being cut off; label as plausible, not confirmed thought.'),('What effect does withholding Lena’s explanation until paragraph 4 have?','Readers initially see her through narrator’s assumption, then reconsider with narrator.'),('Write a claim about the narrator’s growth supported by two details.','From guarding solitude to valuing shared knowledge; cite refusal and shared notebook/arches.'),('Rewrite one sentence from a neutral third-person view. Explain the change.','Example: The bus window faced a wall. Removes possessive/evaluative “precious,” reducing narrator judgment.'),('Is point of view the same as perspective? Explain with this story.','No. First-person form remains; narrator’s outlook on Lena changes.'),('Name the turning-point evidence.','Lena’s labeled drawing and account of her grandfather’s work.'),('Explain how the final sentence reflects a new outlook.','The narrator values another person’s insight alongside personal observation.'),('Support an alternative interpretation: the narrator feels embarrassed.','Can cite change after abrupt dismissal and moving notebook as repair; accept supported inference, not a stated certainty.')],price=600)

add('nj-g2-vowel-trails','Vowel-Team Trails: AI, AY, EE, and EA','New Jersey','2','Phonics / spelling','L.RF.2.3','Decode common long-vowel words with ai, ay, ee, and ea.',NJ+'2.shtml','Phonics task practice',
'In these examples, ai and ay spell long a; ee and ea spell long e. Underline the team, blend the word, and check its meaning. These patterns are common, not universal: ea in bread has a different sound. Teach the listed patterns explicitly; do not teach “two vowels always say the first vowel’s name.”',
[('Read train; underline the team.','ai spells long a in train; blend the beginning tr and ending n around it.'),('Read beach; underline the vowel team.','ea spells long e; ch is a separate consonant digraph.')],
[('Practice bank','ai: rain, mail, tail, paint. ay: day, play, stay, tray. ee: seed, feet, green, sleep. ea: team, leaf, seat, clean. Adult: preteach meanings and consonant clusters as needed, then have learners read the target word independently.')],
[('Read rain and day. What vowel sound do they share?','Long a; ai/ay spell it here.'),('Read seed and leaf. What vowel sound do they share?','Long e; ee/ea spell it here.'),('Sort mail, play, paint, stay by vowel team.','ai: mail, paint. ay: play, stay.'),('Sort green, team, feet, seat by vowel team.','ee: green, feet. ea: team, seat.'),('Read tail. Change t to m. Read the new word.','mail; vowel team and long-a sound stay the same.'),('Read day. Add pl in place of d. Read the new word.','play.'),('Read seed. Change d to m. Read the new word.','seem; long e remains.'),('Read seat. Change t to m. Read the new word.','seam; long e remains.'),('Complete r__n for water falling from clouds: ai or ee?','rain, ai.'),('Complete sl__p for resting at night: ay or ee?','sleep, ee.'),('Complete l__f for part of a plant: ea or ai?','leaf, ea.'),('Complete tr__ for something that carries plates: ay or ee?','tray, ay.'),('Read: The green team can play. Find three vowel-team words.','green (ee), team (ea), play (ay).'),('Read: I can paint a leaf. Find both vowel-team words.','paint (ai), leaf (ea).'),('Write a sentence with clean; read it aloud.','Meaningful sentence with clean accurately spelled and decoded.'),('Does ea always have long e? Use the lesson’s exception.','No. Bread has a different vowel sound.'),('Read stay and identify its vowel team.','ay; long a.'),('Read feet and identify its vowel team.','ee; long e.'),('Complete m__l for letters delivered to a home. Read it.','mail, ai.'),('Read: The team will stay by the green seat. Sort the team words.','ea: team, seat; ay: stay; ee: green. Read each accurately.')],price=450)

add('nj-g6-pronoun-repair','Pronoun Repair Shop: Case and Clarity','New Jersey','6','Grammar','L.SS.6.1','Use subject, object, and possessive pronouns and clarify their referents.',NJ+'6.shtml','Grammar editing workshop',
'Use subject forms for the doer (I, she, he, we, they); object forms after a verb or preposition (me, her, him, us, them); possessive forms to show belonging (my/mine, her/hers, our/ours). For a compound, temporarily remove the other noun to hear the case. Name an unclear referent instead of guessing. This is focused practice within L.SS.6.1.',
[('Choose: Mira and (I/me) prepared the display.','I. Removing Mira leaves “I prepared,” the subject.'),('Repair ambiguity: Sam told Lee that he had the key.','If Sam has it: Sam told Lee, “I have the key.” If Lee has it: Sam told Lee, “You have the key.” State the intended meaning.')],
[('Case and clarity reference','Subject: I, you, he, she, it, we, they. Object: me, you, him, her, it, us, them. Possessive before noun: my, your, his, her, its, our, their. Independent possessive: mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs. Possessive its has no apostrophe; it’s means it is or it has.')],
[('Choose and explain: Leo and (I/me) planned the route.','I; subject performing planned.'),('Choose: The coach thanked Leo and (I/me).','me; object of thanked.'),('Choose: The note is for (she/her).','her; object of for.'),('Choose: (We/Us) organized the folders.','We; subject.'),('Choose: The folders belong to (they/them).','them; object of to.'),('Choose: This seat is (our/ours).','ours; independent possessive, no noun after it.'),('Choose: (Their/Theirs) diagram is complete.','Their; modifies diagram.'),('Fix: Me and Ada checked the list. Explain.','Ada and I checked the list. I is the subject form; placing Ada first is courteous style.'),('Fix: The guide spoke to Ada and I.','The guide spoke to Ada and me; object of to.'),('Fix: That notebook is her.','That notebook is hers; independent possessive.'),('Choose: The robot moved (its/it’s) arm.','its; possession, not it is.'),('Choose: (Its/It’s) time to begin.','It’s; means it is.'),('Clarify: Maya texted Jo after she finished. Assume Maya finished.','After Maya finished, she texted Jo. Referent now explicit.'),('Clarify the same sentence if Jo finished.','After Jo finished, Maya texted her. Explicit Jo resolves the intended meaning.'),('Repair a person shift: When students revise, you should check each claim.','When students revise, they should check each claim. Keeps third-person reference.'),('Write a sentence with a compound object including me.','Example: The leader invited Ana and me. Require me in object position.'),('Choose: Between you and (I/me), the label is unclear.','me; object of between.'),('Fix: Her and Sam built the stand.','She and Sam built the stand. She is subject form.'),('Clarify: Eli handed Max his bag. Assume the bag belongs to Eli.','Eli handed his own bag to Max; or Eli handed Max the bag that belonged to Eli.'),('Write one sentence with their and one with theirs. Explain the difference.','Their poster is ready. The poster is theirs. Their comes before a noun; theirs stands independently.')],price=550)

add('de-g5-time-editor','The Time Editor: Keep Verb Tenses Consistent','Delaware','5','Grammar / writing','CCSS.ELA-Literacy.L.5.1.d','Recognize and repair inappropriate tense shifts while preserving intended time.',DE,'Editing warm-ups',
'Choose a time frame, then keep verbs consistent unless the meaning genuinely moves to another time. Words such as yesterday, now, and tomorrow can justify a shift. Do not change every verb blindly; explain when the event happens. This packet targets tense consistency, not a full verb-tense course.',
[('Fix: Yesterday I pack a bag and walked to school.','Yesterday I packed a bag and walked to school. Both events occurred in the past.'),('Is this shift appropriate: I practiced yesterday, and I will perform tomorrow?','Yes. The time words explicitly separate past practice from future performance.')],
[('Editing checklist','1. Circle a time clue. 2. Identify each verb. 3. Decide whether the time actually changes. 4. Correct only mismatched verbs. 5. Reread for meaning. If no time clue exists, more than one consistent revision may be valid.')],
[('Fix: Last night we watch a film and discussed it.','Last night we watched a film and discussed it.'),('Fix: Every morning Ana opens the window and watered the plant.','Every morning Ana opens the window and waters the plant.'),('Fix: Tomorrow I will label the jars and stored them.','Tomorrow I will label the jars and store them. Will applies to both coordinated verbs.'),('Fix: Yesterday Ben writes a note, folded it, and mailed it.','Yesterday Ben wrote a note, folded it, and mailed it.'),('Is a shift justified: We lived there in 2020, but we live here now? Explain.','Yes; 2020 and now indicate different times.'),('Is a shift justified: Yesterday I bake a loaf and sliced it? Explain.','No; both events yesterday require baked and sliced.'),('Fix: Each week the club meets and planned an activity.','Each week the club meets and plans an activity.'),('Fix: Next Saturday we will sweep and painted the room.','Next Saturday we will sweep and paint the room.'),('Rewrite in past: I draw a map and mark the path.','I drew a map and marked the path.'),('Rewrite in present habit: She carried books and greeted neighbors.','She carries books and greets neighbors.'),('Rewrite in future: They measure and cut the board.','They will measure and cut the board.'),('Keep the real time change: I studied yesterday; today I review. Must both be past?','No. Yesterday studied is past; today review is present. Meaning justifies the shift.'),('Fix consistently: The child opened the box, sees a kite, and smiled.','The child opened the box, saw a kite, and smiled. All past.'),('Fix: Water dripped from the roof while we wait yesterday.','Water dripped from the roof while we waited yesterday.'),('Write a two-sentence past-tense account of preparing a meal.','Require past verbs throughout unless a justified different time is explicit. Any invented meal accepted.'),('Add one sentence about tomorrow without creating an error.','Use an explicit future marker and future verb, e.g., Tomorrow I will try another recipe.'),('Fix: Last week the team designs a logo and printed signs.','Last week the team designed a logo and printed signs.'),('Fix: Every day I checked the shelf and return books.','Every day I check the shelf and return books, for present habit; also accept checked/returned with clearly past habit context.'),('Explain why this works: I knew the route then, but I need a map now.','Then and now justify past knew and present need.'),('Write three connected sentences: past, present, future. Mark time clues.','Example: Yesterday I planned. Today I gather supplies. Tomorrow I will build. Each time clue matches its tense.')],extraSources=['https://www.thecorestandards.org/ELA-Literacy/L/5/'],price=500)

add('de-g6-purpose-lens','Purpose Lens: Two Notices, Different Aims','Delaware','6','Reading informational text','CCSS.ELA-Literacy.RI.6.6','Explain an author’s purpose or viewpoint through wording and selected details.',DE,'Reading small-group analysis',
'Purpose is what a writer seeks to do: explain, inform, persuade, or another goal. Viewpoint is the writer’s position or outlook. Identify audience, word choice, and selection of details. A text can inform while also trying to persuade. These notices describe an invented community event; they are not real announcements.',
[('“Bring a bowl; soup service begins at noon.” What is the main purpose?','Give practical instructions/information; concrete action and time cues support that reading.'),('“Join us—the neighborhood needs your ideas.” What adds persuasion?','Direct invitation and an appeal to the audience’s contribution, not merely logistical information.')],
[('A. Event notice (fictional)','The Maple Room will host a repair exchange on Saturday from 10 a.m. to noon. Volunteers will demonstrate button sewing and bicycle-tire patching on sample materials. Visitors may observe or practice on the samples. No electrical appliances will be repaired. A registration sheet at the front desk has space for twenty visitors. The room has a step-free entrance. Anyone who needs a different way to participate may contact the desk before Friday.'),('B. Organizer’s invitation (fictional)','A missing button need not end a favorite shirt’s story. Join our Saturday repair exchange and discover a skill you can use again. You do not need to arrive knowing how: volunteers will guide the practice. Our twenty places let us keep groups small enough for questions. Watch first if you prefer, then try a sample when you feel ready. Bring your curiosity, not a broken appliance. Let’s make repair a shared neighborhood habit.')],
[('Who is a likely audience for both texts?','Potential community visitors, including beginners.'),('State A’s main purpose and cite a detail.','Inform/guide attendance; dates, times, activities, limits, registration.'),('State B’s main purpose and cite wording.','Persuade people to attend and value repair; “Join,” “discover,” shared habit.'),('What outlook toward repair does B convey?','Positive and encouraging; treats repair as reusable skill and community habit.'),('Why does A name activities and exclusions?','Helps visitors understand scope and avoid bringing unsuitable items.'),('How does B address a beginner’s hesitation?','Says no prior knowledge needed and volunteers guide practice; allows watching first.'),('Compare how each text presents twenty places.','A factual capacity/registration; B frames small groups as useful for questions.'),('What effect does “a shirt’s story” have?','Personifies/figuratively gives emotional value to keeping a shirt rather than replacing it.'),('Is A entirely unrelated to participation? Explain.','No. Its information makes participation possible; dominant purpose is logistical guidance.'),('Which notice gives an exact end time?','A: noon.'),('Which gives a reason to value the event beyond that day?','B: skills reusable later and a shared neighborhood habit.'),('Does B prove repair always saves money?','No. It makes no measured cost comparison and does not establish an always claim.'),('How does direct address “you” affect B?','Invites reader personally and lowers distance, supporting persuasion.'),('Identify a detail in A that may help someone plan access.','Step-free entrance or contacting desk for another participation method.'),('Rewrite one line from B in a neutral informational style.','Example: Volunteers will guide visitors using sample materials. Removes invitation/evaluative language.'),('Write a three-sentence comparison of purposes using one detail from each.','Require A logistical focus, B persuasive focus, two accurate details, and explanation rather than labels alone.'),('A new line says: “Don’t miss your chance to become part of the solution.” Is it neutral?','No; urgency and valued identity are persuasive.'),('Write a neutral version of that line.','Example: Registration is available at the front desk. No pressure or unsupported promise.'),('Could A and B share facts but differ in purpose? Explain.','Yes. The same capacity/activity details can inform or be framed as reasons to attend.'),('Which two textual features would you examine in a new notice? Why?','Audience/direct address, selected details, evaluative words, calls to action. Any two with how they reveal purpose/viewpoint.')],extraSources=['https://www.thecorestandards.org/ELA-Literacy/RI/6/'],price=550)

add('nc-g7-word-choice','When the Hallway Wakes: Poetry Word-Choice Lab','North Carolina','7','Poetry / reading','RL.7.4','Interpret figurative language and analyze how specific words shape meaning and tone.',NC+'cm205-gr7-understandingelascos/open','Poetry task practice',
'Name a device only after interpreting its meaning. Explain what a word adds that a neutral substitute would lose. Tone is the speaker’s attitude, not merely the reader’s feeling. These original short poems are focused teaching texts, not full-length EOG simulations. The released EOG form informed text-dependent question design; no released content is reproduced.',
[('“The clock scolded the silent room.” Explain, then name the device.','Ticking seems accusatory or impatient; giving a clock a human action is personification. Scolded creates tension.'),('Replace scolded with ticked. What changes?','The literal action remains, but the blaming/impatient attitude largely disappears.')],
[('A. Before the Bell','[1] The hallway holds its breath at dawn.\n[2] A broom whispers along the floor.\n[3] My unfinished question rides my tongue,\n[4] a folded kite beside a door.\n\n[5] Then sneakers scatter little storms;\n[6] bright voices loosen every knot.\n[7] I lift my hand, release the string—\n[8] the question flies from its small spot.'),('B. After the Fair','[1] The gym exhales its paper stars.\n[2] We stack the chairs in patient rows.\n[3] A ribbon sleeps beside the door;\n[4] nobody hurries when we close.\n\n[5] One crooked sign remains on tape,\n[6] still pointing toward an empty stand.\n[7] I smooth its corner, fold it twice,\n[8] and carry evening in my hand.')],
[('A1: Explain “holds its breath.”','Hallway is quiet and expectant before activity; personification, not literal breathing.'),('A2: What does whispers suggest about the broom sound?','Soft, low movement; quieter than scrapes/crashes.'),('A3–4: What is compared to a folded kite?','The unspoken/unfinished question, waiting to be released.'),('A5: Explain “little storms.”','Busy, noisy movement of students; metaphor, not actual weather.'),('A6: What might “knots” represent?','Nervousness or difficulty speaking; voices ease tension in context.'),('A7–8: Explain the extended kite image.','Lifting a hand/asking releases the question like a kite; image develops from folded to flying.'),('A: How does tone shift between stanzas?','From quiet hesitation/anticipation to lively confidence; cite breath/unfinished versus lift/flies.'),('A2: Replace whispers with pounds. How would tone change?','More loud/forceful or harsh; soft quiet atmosphere would weaken.'),('B1: Explain “exhales.”','The gym seems to release energy after the event; personification suggests winding down.'),('B2: What does patient add to rows?','Calm, unhurried order; gives the arrangement an almost human quality.'),('B3: Explain ribbon sleeps.','Ribbon lies still/unused; personification reinforces rest after activity.'),('B6: How does empty affect the scene?','Emphasizes the event is over and people/items have left; supports quiet reflection.'),('B8: Interpret “carry evening in my hand.”','The folded sign carries a memory or trace of the evening; cannot literally hold the time of day.'),('Compare the connotations of scatter in A and stack in B.','Scatter suggests lively dispersal; stack suggests orderly cleanup; actions support contrasting energy.'),('Which poem feels more reflective? Support an interpretation.','B, with no hurry, empty stand, and carefully folded sign; accept nuanced alternative with strong evidence.'),('Write two sentences linking one device to the poem’s meaning.','Require accurate figurative interpretation plus effect on speaking/growing confidence in A or closure/memory in B.'),('A4: Why folded rather than flying at this point?','Question is still unspoken; folded prepares for the later release.'),('B2: Replace patient with rigid. Explain the difference.','Rigid suggests stiffness/strictness; patient suggests calm waiting.'),('Name a word supporting each poem’s tone and explain it.','A flies: confidence/freedom; B sleeps: rest. Any justified pairing accepted.'),('Write a 3–4-sentence analysis of how A’s kite image develops.','Trace folded question to loosened knots/lift/release/flies, explain transition from hesitation to expression, cite line details.')],price=600)

add('nc-g8-claim-check','The Quiet Room Proposal: Claims and Evidence Lab','North Carolina','8','Reading / argument analysis','RI.8.8','Evaluate claims, reasoning, and the relevance and sufficiency of evidence.',NC+'cm206-gr8-understandingelascos/open','Small-group argument lab',
'Trace claim → reason → evidence → warrant (why evidence supports claim). Relevant evidence concerns the claim; sufficient evidence is enough in quality and scope to justify it. An anecdote may illustrate a point without proving a broad conclusion. All names, counts, and surveys below are invented classroom data; no real school research is claimed.',
[('Three volunteers like a pilot activity. Does that prove all students prefer it?','No. Small self-selected participation is relevant to those volunteers but insufficient for all students.'),('Would a building’s paint color prove a quiet room improves focus?','No. Unless a relevant connection is established, paint color does not address that learning claim.')],
[('Proposal (fictional)','[1] Our school should pilot a supervised quiet room during one lunch period for four weeks. Students could choose it on days they want a lower-noise setting. The goal is another option, not replacing social lunch.\n[2] In an invented survey, 48 of 60 responding students said they would use such a room at least once. The survey was sent only to members of the homework club; the school has 480 students. This proves that nearly everyone at school wants a quiet room.\n[3] A three-day trial involved twelve volunteers. Nine reported that they finished more of their chosen reading than during their usual lunch setting. They selected their own books and reported their own progress. No comparison group or objective reading measure was used. The trial therefore guarantees higher reading scores for every student.\n[4] Supervision would require one available adult and a room approved by school leaders. We propose tracking use and inviting feedback from students who do not attend as well as those who do. We should check staffing before opening.\n[5] Our school won an art award last spring, so the quiet room will succeed. The award certificate has a gold border. A limited pilot with clear feedback questions would let us test whether the room is useful without assuming the answer.')],
[('State the main proposal with its limits.','A supervised optional quiet-room pilot during one lunch period for four weeks.'),('What reason is offered for the room?','Provide a lower-noise lunch option, not replace social lunch.'),('What population actually received the survey?','Homework club members, not the whole school.'),('What fraction/percent of respondents said they would use it?','48/60 = 4/5 = 80% of respondents, not 80% of all 480 students.'),('Is paragraph 2’s “nearly everyone” conclusion justified?','No. Nonrepresentative surveyed group and limited responses cannot establish whole-school preference.'),('Rewrite that claim accurately.','Forty-eight of sixty homework-club respondents said they would use the room at least once.'),('What did the trial actually measure?','Volunteers’ self-reports of reading progress, not standardized reading scores.'),('Why are different self-selected books a limitation?','Task difficulty/length may differ, weakening direct comparisons of amount read.'),('What does the missing comparison group prevent us from concluding?','Cannot isolate the room as the cause of reported progress relative to other conditions.'),('Evaluate “guarantees higher reading scores for every student.”','Unsupported overgeneralization: short small volunteer trial, self-report, no score data, no comparison.'),('Identify relevant planning evidence in paragraph 4.','Staff availability and approved room are relevant to feasibility.'),('Why seek feedback from nonattenders?','Can reveal barriers or unmet needs missed by feedback only from users.'),('Which paragraph 5 detail is clearly irrelevant? Explain.','Gold border/art award does not establish quiet-room effectiveness; no logical link shown.'),('Can relevant evidence still be insufficient? Use the trial.','Yes. Reports concern reading but are too limited to prove broad causal or score claims.'),('Suggest one stronger evidence collection plan.','Compare defined outcomes before/during pilot with wider participation or suitable comparison, record limitations; no guarantee of causality from simple counts.'),('Write a qualified recommendation in three sentences.','May support a limited pilot if staffing/approval secured; accurately cite survey/trial; acknowledge limits and request broader evidence.'),('Which claim can the data directly support: nine volunteers reported progress; all students gained skills?','Nine volunteers reported progress; no measured universal gains.'),('Explain why the award is not sufficient evidence.','It concerns an unrelated achievement, not this program’s outcomes or feasibility.'),('Name one missing piece needed to assess schoolwide demand.','Representative or schoolwide response data, including non-homework-club students and nonresponse limits.'),('Evaluate the proposal without rejecting every part.','Separate reasonable optional pilot/staffing plan from unsupported universal claims; cite relevant data and limitations.')],price=600)

add('college-french-negation','French 101: Say What Is Not Happening','', 'College','French','Course objective','Form and interpret present-tense negation with ne/n’ … pas.', 'https://laits.utexas.edu/tex/gr/neg2.html','Language practice packet',
'In the written forms practiced here, place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. Before a vowel or mute h, ne becomes n’. Keep the verb conjugation. Ordinary spoken French sometimes drops ne; this packet practices the full written form. The supplied sentences avoid indefinite/partitive-object changes so the focus stays on placement. This independent French 101 resource claims no accreditation, transfer, or equivalency.',
[('Negate: Tu parles français.','Tu ne parles pas français. Put ne/pas around parles.'),('Negate: Elle aime lire.','Elle n’aime pas lire. Aime starts with a vowel; lire remains an infinitive after the conjugated verb.')],
[('Support bank','je = I; tu = you (singular informal); il/elle = he/she; nous = we; vous = you (formal/plural); ils/elles = they. parler = speak; travailler = work; habiter = live; aimer = like; écouter = listen; lire = read. aujourd’hui = today; ici = here; demain = tomorrow; souvent = often. Keep definite le/la/les in these examples.')],
[('Negate: Je parle allemand.','Je ne parle pas allemand.'),('Negate: Tu travailles ici.','Tu ne travailles pas ici.'),('Negate: Elle habite à Paris.','Elle n’habite pas à Paris. H in habite is mute.'),('Negate: Nous aimons le théâtre.','Nous n’aimons pas le théâtre.'),('Negate: Vous écoutez la radio.','Vous n’écoutez pas la radio.'),('Negate: Ils arrivent demain.','Ils n’arrivent pas demain.'),('Negate: Je suis prêt.','Je ne suis pas prêt.'),('Negate: Elles sont ici.','Elles ne sont pas ici.'),('Fix: Tu pas parles français.','Tu ne parles pas français.'),('Fix: Elle ne aime pas lire.','Elle n’aime pas lire.'),('Fix: Nous ne travaillons ici pas.','Nous ne travaillons pas ici.'),('Fix: Je n’suis pas prêt.','Je ne suis pas prêt. Suis starts with a consonant sound.'),('Translate: Vous ne travaillez pas aujourd’hui.','You are not working today / You do not work today. Vous may be formal singular or plural.'),('Translate: Ils n’habitent pas ici.','They do not live here.'),('Write the negative in French: She likes the music. Use Elle aime la musique.','Elle n’aime pas la musique. Keep la, a definite article.'),('Explain why n’ appears in n’écoutez but ne appears in ne parlez.','Écoutez begins with a vowel; parlez begins with a consonant sound.'),('Negate: Nous sommes prêts.','Nous ne sommes pas prêts.'),('Negate: Tu aimes le cinéma.','Tu n’aimes pas le cinéma.'),('Translate: Je ne parle pas espagnol.','I do not speak Spanish.'),('Negate: Elles écoutent la musique. Explain placement.','Elles n’écoutent pas la musique. N’ precedes the vowel-initial conjugated verb; pas follows it.')],price=600)

add('college-speech-openings','Public Speaking 101: Openings That Orient','', 'College','Communications / public speaking','Course objective','Design an accurate informative-speech opening for a defined audience.', 'https://www.unr.edu/writing-speaking-center/writing-speaking-resources/speech-introductions','Speech planning workshop',
'An informative opening draws attention, names the topic, shows audience relevance, establishes an honest basis for speaking, and previews the route. Do not invent personal experience, statistics, credentials, or sources. A hypothetical situation must be labeled hypothetical. This introductory college practice has no accreditation or universal course-equivalency claim.',
[('Improve “Today I talk about files” for students losing drafts.','Imagine opening three files called final. Today I will demonstrate a naming routine for class drafts: a date, a topic, and a version. The imagined problem connects to the audience and previews three parts.'),('Can a student say “Research proves this always works” without a source?','No. Replace it with a bounded demonstration claim: I will show one organizing method you can test.')],
[('Fictional speaking brief','Audience: first-year students who write on shared computers. Purpose: explain a file-naming routine, not endorse an app. Demonstration: use YYYY-MM-DD_topic_v01, increase the version number after major revisions, and keep the class’s required submission name for the final upload. A filename alone is not a backup. The assignment is a 60–90-second opening, not a full technical-security lesson. The speaker may honestly say they practiced this demonstration if they actually did.'),('Sample opening for analysis','Imagine finding four documents called final before class. Which one contains your latest revision? Today I will show a simple naming routine for working drafts. I have prepared two sample files for this demonstration. We will label the date, add a topic, and update the version number. Keep your instructor’s required filename for submission; this routine is for organizing your working copies.')],
[('Name the specific audience and one likely need.','First-year students using shared computers; distinguishing drafts without assuming they are all inexperienced.'),('Is the sample hook a factual statistic? Explain.','No. It is explicitly an imagined scenario.'),('Identify the topic statement.','Today I will show a simple naming routine for working drafts.'),('Identify the preview’s three steps.','Label date, add topic, update version number.'),('What is the limited credibility claim?','Speaker prepared two sample files; true only if actually prepared.'),('How should a speaker revise that claim if no files were prepared?','Prepare the files before speaking or remove/rewrite honestly; never claim nonexistent work.'),('What boundary about submission is stated?','Follow instructor’s required filename for submission; routine applies to working copies.'),('Why is “This method prevents every lost file” inaccurate?','Naming helps organization but does not guarantee saving, backups, permissions, or recovery.'),('Write an audience-relevant question hook without inventing a statistic.','Example: How do you tell which draft you revised most recently? Relevant and not falsely quantified.'),('Write a neutral informative purpose statement.','Example: I will explain a three-part draft filename. Not “I will prove this is the only good system.”'),('Write a three-part preview in logical order.','Date, topic, version; preserve clear sequence or justify another coherent order.'),('Add one limitation without derailing the opening.','Example: Names help us identify files, but we still need a backup. Accurate and brief.'),('Rewrite the hook for an audience already using version control.','Acknowledge experience and focus on readable exported/submitted drafts; avoid implying no existing skill.'),('Draft a 60–90-second opening on separate paper.','Require attention, topic, audience relevance, honest credibility, preview, and appropriate limit. Time actual delivery; word count alone is not proof.'),('Read it aloud and record elapsed time. Revise if needed.','Accept actual measured duration plus cuts/additions that maintain essential components; no invented time.'),('Ask a listener to repeat the three steps. Record and use feedback.','Record what listener actually recalls; revise unclear preview or signposting. Solo alternative: pause then reconstruct outline before checking.') ,('New topic: demonstrate how to label a classroom plant-care log. Write an honest hook.','Relevant question or labeled hypothetical, no fabricated research/statistics.'),('State the topic and audience benefit.','Explain recording date, observation, and action so classmates can interpret the log; no guaranteed plant-health claim.'),('Write a three-part preview and an honest credibility line.','Preview date/observation/action; say you prepared a sample only if true, or state what will be demonstrated without credential claims.'),('Give a short opening and self-assess against five components.','Attention, topic, relevance, honest basis, preview all present. Accept written/audio/live format; evaluate organization and accuracy, not accent or eye-contact norms.')],price=650)

